1
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:06,880
In this last part of the lecture, 
let's focus on how the OS itself can  

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,440
act as an arbitrator to avoid deadlocks. 

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:14,800
In the examples we have seen so far, all the 
threads trying to access shared resources sort  

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:20,480
of know each other, they share variables, they 
have clear roles (e.g., readers and writers),  

5
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:24,560
and so on. But the threads accessing 
resources on a computer might be  

6
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:29,840
unaware of which other threads are running, what 
resources the latter want to access, and so on...

7
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:34,800
In this sense, the OS needs to monitor 
everything that is happening and,  

8
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:38,400
if it detects that the requests of 
a thread could lead to a deadlock,  

9
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:43,040
do something about it, for instance, 
decide to schedule the execution of  

10
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:46,160
such a thread for later on.
We are going to introduce a  

11
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:51,360
model for this setup and also mention the 
Banker's algorithm (introduced by Dijkstra).

12
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:56,800
Let's begin with the system model, 
which in this case relies on a graph. 

13
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:01,920
In this graph, we have two types of 
vertexes: resources and processes  

14
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:05,840
(this is a bipartite graph).
A resource can refer to CPU,  

15
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:12,320
memory, I/O devices, and so on.
Each resource Ri has Wi instances

16
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:19,040
Since this is a graph, we also have edges. 
Each edge connects a Resource with a Process.  

17
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:25,040
If the edge/arrow starts at the process and 
ends at a Resource, it means the process is  

18
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:32,080
requesting the resource. If the edge/arrow starts 
at a resource and ends at a process, it means the  

19
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:37,680
resource is assigned to the process.
In the case of a request edge,  

20
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:42,240
we use a dashed line to differentiate an 
actual request from a potential request  

21
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:48,240
(i.e., a dashed line implies the process might 
request the resource but has not done yet).

22
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:54,720
In our model, besides knowing which resources and 
processes we have, and how many instances we have,  

23
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:59,440
we also know the maximum number of instances 
each process may need for a given resource.

24
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:08,000
When it comes to the OS, the idea is that the OS 
should be able to distinguish which portion of its  

25
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,400
decision space is safe and which is not (in other 
words, which decisions might lead to a deadlock).  

26
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:20,000
If, by deciding to give control to 
a certain process, the OS allows  

27
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,120
for such a process to request a resource 
instance that can lead to a deadlock,  

28
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:30,960
then what the OS can do is to instead suspend 
such a process, until enough resources are freed.

29
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:38,720
Suppose for instance we are in this situation: 
processes P1 and P2 could be executed next.  

30
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:46,640
Currently, R1 has been given to P1 and P2 
has requested R1. The OS also knows that  

31
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:53,280
both P1 and P2 might request R2. Which 
process is safe to run next? P1 or P2? 

32
00:02:54,800 --> 00:03:00,480
If the OS decides to run P1, 
P1 might request and get R2,  

33
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:06,560
which is not leading to any deadlock (so from a 
safe state the OS switches to another safe state). 

34
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:14,720
If the OS decides to run P2, P2 might 
request and get R2, which in this case  

35
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:19,920
creates a circular loop, and this might 
mean deadlock (so from a safe state the  

36
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:24,240
OS switches to an unsafe state)
Based on these observations,  

37
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:31,840
the OS can decide to run P1 and maybe suspend 
P2 until such a situation can no longer happen.

38
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:36,960
For the model we introduced, Dijkstra 
introduced an algorithm for resource  

39
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:41,760
allocation that can avoid deadlocks.
The idea is to maintain three data structures: 

40
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:47,520
a matrix called max, that in each position 
i j contains the maximum number of instances  

41
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:54,000
process i can request for resource j
a matrix called allocation, that in each  

42
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,840
position i j contains the number of instances 
already allocated to process i of resource j 

43
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:06,720
an array called available, that in position 
j contains the number of instances that are  

44
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:11,520
still available for resource j.
As mentioned before, the idea is  

45
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:15,600
to check whether potentially satisfying 
a request can lead to an unsafe state.  

46
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:21,360
How is the check done? By checking whether, by 
satisfying a request, the other processes can  

47
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:26,560
still obtain all the resources they might request 
or not. If that is the case, the request can be  

48
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:31,840
satisfied, otherwise, the requesting process is 
suspended until enough resources are free-ed.

49
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,040
Please notice that the OS has an alternative 
to prevent a deadlock from happening:  

50
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:43,280
that of being able to detect a deadlock 
as happened and then resolve it.  

51
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:48,720
In other words, the OS can decide to 
be reactive rather than proactive.

52
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:54,480
In that case, there can be two ways out. 
The first one is to terminate the process  

53
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:59,520
that has caused a deadlock. Eventually, 
once one or more processes are terminated,  

54
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:04,400
enough resources will be available and the 
remaining processes will be able to continue.

55
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:10,000
The second option is to select victim 
processes and rollback their execution,  

56
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,680
basically go back in their execution to a point 
in which the requests made by them did not result  

57
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:20,160
in a deadlock and start again their execution 
later on when more resources are available.

58
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:27,040
Usually, process recovery is a costly operation, 
which should be avoided to the extent possible.  

59
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:31,280
As always, nonetheless, the exact costs 
of each alternative (including both  

60
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:37,440
proactive and reactive ones) will depend 
on the specific case taken into account!

